<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wyke–Cruz Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wyke–Cruz Treaty was signed in November 1859 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, represented by Charles Lennox Wyke, and Honduras, represented by Francisco Cruz. Britain recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-islands">Bay Islands</a> as Honduran territory and handed them over in 1860, in line with its commitments under the Clayton–Bulwer Treaty, while Honduras guaranteed the islanders' property and religious freedom. It also recognised Honduran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over its part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquito Coast</a>.</p></div>
